[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Services, Foreground Services, and WorkManager for Background Tasks in Android (Kotlin)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Managing background operations effectively is crucial for building responsive and battery-efficient Android applications. In modern Android development, background tasks are handled using different components depending on their urgency, duration, and whether they need to survive process death. This tutorial explores standard Services, Foreground Services, and WorkManager."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Background Processing Options"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Services: Basic components for running operations without a UI. Note that background services are heavily restricted in modern Android versions."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Foreground Services: Used for tasks that the user needs to be actively aware of, such as playing music or tracking a workout. They require a persistent notification."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"WorkManager: The recommended API for persistent, deferrable background work that needs to run even if the app is closed or the device restarts."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing a Foreground Service"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Foreground Service must display a notification to the user. First, declare the permission in your AndroidManifest.xml:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, implement the Service class in Kotlin:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"class MyForegroundService : Service() {
    companion5 object {
        const val CHANNEL_ID = "ForegroundServiceChannel"
    }

    override fun onStartCommand(intent: Intent?, flags: Int, startId: Int): Int {
        createNotificationChannel()
        val notification = NotificationCompat.Builder(this, CHANNEL_ID)
            .setContentTitle("Foreground Service")
            .setContentText("Running background sync...")
            .setSmallIcon(R.drawable.ic_notification)
            .build()

        startForeground(1, notification)

        // Perform long running task here

        return START_NOT_STICKY
    }

    override fun onBind(intent: Intent?): IBinder? = null

    private fun createNotificationChannel() {
        if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= Build.VERSION_CODES.O) {
            val serviceChannel = NotificationChannel(
                CHANNEL_ID,
                "Foreground Service Channel",
                NotificationManager.IMPORTANCE_DEFAULT
            )
            val manager = getSystemService(NotificationManager::class.java)
            manager?.createNotificationChannel(serviceChannel)
        }
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Using WorkManager for Reliable Background Tasks"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"For tasks that do not require immediate execution but must finish reliably (like uploading logs or syncing data), WorkManager is the best choice. Add the dependency to your build.gradle:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"implementation "androidx.work:work-runtime-ktx:2.9.0""},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a Worker class by extending Worker:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"class SyncWorker(context: Context, params: WorkerParameters) : Worker(context, params) {
    override fun doWork(): Result {
        return try {
            // Perform network request or heavy operation
            Thread.sleep(3000)
            Result.success()
        } catch (e: Exception) {
            Result.failure()
        }
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Finally, enqueue the WorkRequest with specific constraints:"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"val constraints = Constraints.Builder()
    .setRequiredNetworkType(NetworkType.CONNECTED)
    .setRequiresCharging(true)
    .build()

val syncWorkRequest = OneTimeWorkRequestBuilder<SyncWorker>()
    .setConstraints(constraints)
    .build()

WorkManager.getInstance(context).enqueue(syncWorkRequest)"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right tool is vital. Use WorkManager for deferrable, guaranteed background tasks, Foreground Services for active user-facing operations, and avoid legacy background services to comply with modern Android battery management guidelines."}]
